{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part6.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock is Historically Cheap. The Decade-Low Multiple Reflects Risks, But This Looks Like a Can't-Miss Buy Opportunity for NVDA.\",\n   \"publisher\": \"barchart.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia Stock is Historically Cheap. The Decade-Low Multiple Reflects Risks, But This Looks Like a Can't-Miss Buy Opportunity for NVDA.\\nNvidia (NVDA) stock is currently trading at less than 17 times expected forward 12-month earnings, representing the cheapest valuation the shares have commanded in over a decade and roughly half the multiple investors were willing to pay as recently as early 2025.\\nThis dramatic de-rating has occurred against a backdrop of accelerating, not decelerating, fundamentals — a paradox that sits at the heart of the debate over whether NVDA's compressed multiple signals heightened risk or a rare value buying opportunity.\\nSpaceX Has Huge, Unusual Put Options Volume - Is SPCX Set to Fall or Rise From Here?\\nLucid Just Struck a Partnership With Bolt on Mobility. What This Means for LCID Stock.\\nThe disconnect between Nvidia's soaring earnings power and its shrinking valuation multiple suggests the market is pricing in meaningful skepticism about the sustainability of the current AI spending cycle.\\nThe fundamental case for Nvidia remains extraordinarily strong. Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion, and management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth.\\nCEO Jensen Huang has projected that chip sales could double in calendar year 2027, while CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled. There has never been a company of Nvidia's scale growing at anything close to this rate.\\nYet several legitimate risks explain the market's caution. Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\\nOther tangible headwinds include calls from leading artificial intelligence (AI) labs, including Anthropic and OpenAI, to slow frontier development; rising community backlash against data center construction; soaring interest rates that pressure hyperscaler capital budgets; and Nvidia's complete exclusion from the Chinese data center market.\\nAdditionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\nThe valuation math, however, is difficult to ignore. At 14 times next fiscal year's consensus earnings estimate, Nvidia trades at a fraction of its 10-year average price/earnings (P/E) ratio of 61 – and well below semiconductor peers like Advanced Micro Devices (AMD) at over 200 times trailing earnings and Broadcom (AVGO) at 46 times.\\nAnalysts remain overwhelmingly bullish, with 45 of 50 firms rating the stock a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" a mean price target near $326 implying 42% upside from current levels, and earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months.\\nIf Nvidia simply maintains its current trailing P/E of 28 while delivering on consensus earnings projections, the stock would roughly double over the next 12 to 18 months.\\nThe decade-low multiple is not so much a red flag as it is the market's attempt to reconcile unprecedented growth with unprecedented uncertainty about how long that growth can last. For investors with a multi-year horizon and a tolerance for volatility tied to AI spending cycles, the risk-reward profile at current levels tilts decisively toward opportunity rather than warning.\\nThis article was created with the support of automated content tools from our partners at Sigma.AI. Together, our financial data and AI solutions help us to deliver more informed market headline analysis to readers faster than ever.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the 45% revenue growth expected for fiscal 2028 before the guide\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the 70% revenue growth guided for fiscal 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Immense Dividend Hike Puts Dividend Growth Stocks in the Spotlight\",\n   \"publisher\": \"morningstar.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Is Nvidia a True Dividend Growth Stock?\\nWhat’s the Big Deal About Dividend Growth Anyway?\\nWhy Are Dividend Growth Stocks Performing Well in 2026?\\nWhat Does Nvidia Joining the Dividend Growth Party Mean?\\nNvidia’s Immense Dividend Hike Puts Dividend Growth Stocks in the Spotlight\\nCompanies growing their dividends have posted strong returns this year. Why is that?\\nLost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement. In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\\nAnalysts will be quick to point out that perspective is key. “Nvidia’s dividend is virtually immaterial relative to its financial health and forward prospects,” wrote Brian Colello , who covers semiconductor stocks for Morningstar. Nvidia’s dividend yield will continue to register well below 1%. And even more of the company’s cash is being spent buying back shares.\\nBut in absolute terms, Nvidia’s dividend is huge. Something like $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders. That sum will make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US stock market.\\nThe Nvidia news also comes at a time when the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index is neck and neck with the broad US stock market, which is unusual in an up year. So, what’s going on with dividend growth stocks this year? And what does Nvidia’s dividend hike suggest, if anything?\\nDespite the dividend increase, it’s probably too soon to call Nvidia a dividend growth stock. Although the company initiated a quarterly dividend back in 2013, the payout was paltry until the 2026 increase. The Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index requires five consecutive years of dividend growth. Other dividend indexes on the market require an even longer history. Active managers running dividend growth strategies interpret their mandates in different ways.\\n“Most of Nvidia’s distributions to shareholders come in the form of share repurchases,” writes Colello. That’s consistent with the overall US stock market trend. As I wrote about recently , companies have spent far more buying back their own shares than they have on dividends.\\nIndeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration. The company’s preference when it comes to cash allocations is clear in the exhibit below.\\nSource: Nvidia 10-K and 10-Q statements reconciled with Morningstar Indexes data by analyst Aryan Singh. Data as of July 26, 2026. Trailing 12-month data used. Buybacks depicted on a gross basis. Download CSV .\\nDividend growth has long been viewed as a symbol of corporate “quality.” Only profitable companies secure in their financial positions consistently increase cash returned to shareholders. While share buybacks can be opportunistic, dividends are a commitment. The market typically punishes dividend cutters.\\nThe nearly 400 stocks in the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index include a mix of market leaders and less familiar names. Mega-caps like Microsoft MSFT , JPMorgan Chase JPM , Johnson & Johnson JNJ , and ExxonMobil XOM , which sit atop the index because constituents are weighted by market capitalization, are joined by mid- and small-cap stocks like Becton Dickinson BDX , DTE Energy DTE , and American Financial Group AFG .\\nInvestment style-wise, the dividend growth index leans toward value. Technology stocks, which tend to carry higher-than-average multiples, represent just 17.6% of index weight. That’s less than half their share of the broad US stock market. Meanwhile, the generally lower-priced financial services, healthcare, industrials, and consumer defensive sectors are overrepresented among dividend growers.\\nWhat about performance? Dividend growers have a reputation for being defensive stocks. Strong balance sheets and moderate price tags mean that dividend growers often shine during risk-off market environments.\\nIndeed, the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index has generally outperformed during selloffs. It declined by less than the broad US stock market in down years 2018 and 2022. It also held its value relatively well during the tariff turmoil of March-April 2025 and in negative months like March and June 2026. From a volatility perspective, the dividend growth index has registered a standard deviation of returns well below the market.\\nGiven its defensive profile, it’s somewhat surprising that the dividend growth index is performing well in 2026. After all, the market is up double digits as the fourth quarter approaches. The technology sector has led this year, powered by the AI buildout. Those conditions aren’t typically favorable to dividend growers.\\nThe energy sector helps explain dividend growth’s strong returns. With the conflict in the Middle East pushing up oil prices, energy companies are booking huge profits. Key index constituents include ExxonMobil, with a near four-decade-long history of dividend growth, and ConocoPhillips COP , which rebuilt its dividend growth track record after low oil prices forced a dividend cut in 2016.\\nBut the biggest contributor to 2026 performance—both in absolute and relative terms—is the healthcare sector. Johnson & Johnson, Merck MRK , and UnitedHealth UNH are all having stellar years. J&J is a paragon of dividend growth going back to the 1960s. Merck and UnitedHealth have more modest histories in the neighborhood of 15 years, but UnitedHealth has posted double-digit dividend growth rates.\\nThe strong year for dividend growth reflects a number of trends. First, market leadership broadened from the narrow group of Magnificent Seven companies that dominated 2023-25. Nvidia, for example, has performed well, but not at the same level as in recent years. Amazon.com AMZN , Alphabet GOOGL , and Meta Platforms META have lagged. Second, this has been a year in which value stocks have outperformed growth. That includes the energy and healthcare sectors, but also value technology stocks benefiting from investments. Third, it has generally been a strong year for companies returning cash to shareholders, as I wrote about recently .\\nActually, this isn’t the first time dividend growers have outperformed in an up market. The dividend growth index also posted stellar returns in 2021, another year of market “breadth.” All US sectors posted strong returns then, and little separated growth and value stocks.\\nSome might see Nvidia’s dividend hike as a sign the company is entering a new, more slow-growth life stage. That doesn’t fit with the 70% revenue growth forecast. One thing that’s certain is that Nvidia’s cash pile is massive.\\nNvidia is also part of a trend I’ve written about regarding tech and tech-adjacent companies paying more dividends. Microsoft and Apple AAPL have both been constituents of the dividend growth index for years. In 2024, Meta and Alphabet both initiated payouts to shareholders.\\nNvidia also fits the dividend growth profile when it comes to profitability and financial strength. Increasing a payout can be seen as a sign of corporate quality. Because of their defensive characteristics, dividend growers are a good option for investors concerned about a market bubble .\\nMorningstar, Inc., licenses indexes to financial institutions as the tracking indexes for investable products, such as exchange-traded funds, sponsored by the financial institution. The license fee for such use is paid by the sponsoring financial institution based mainly on the total assets of the investable product. A list of ETFs that track a Morningstar index is available via the Capabilities section at indexes.morningstar.com. A list of other investable products linked to a Morningstar index is available upon request. Morningstar, Inc., does not market, sell, or make any representations regarding the advisability of investing in any investable product that tracks a Morningstar index.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"about $25 billion a year returned through the dividend\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the 2,400% increase of the dividend\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 3,\n     \"text\": \"the $0.25 quarterly dividend\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"title\": \"Sandisk vs. NVIDIA: Which AI Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Lately, surging artificial intelligence (AI) and data center demand has benefited both NVIDIA Corporation NVDA and the much smaller Sandisk Corporation SNDK. While the two companies offer different ways to capitalize on the AI boom, which one stands out to be the better buy now? Let's explore –\\nSandisk's AI-Driven Growth Boosts Revenues and Profitability\\nTotal revenues for Sandisk reached $8.97 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026, up a whopping 372% year over year and 51% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 5 press release.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, revenues reached $20.25 billion, representing 175% year-over-year growth. The Data Center segment has been the major growth driver, with revenues jumping 437% as AI and data center demand increased the need for high-value storage.\\nIn the fourth fiscal quarter, revenue growth came more from higher pricing than higher volumes. As a result, gross margins rose to 84.6% compared to 26.2% a year ago.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, gross margin was 71.5% compared to 30.1% a year earlier. Profitability also improved dramatically, with GAAP net income coming in at $11.43 billion versus a loss of $1.64 billion in the previous year.\\nThe growth doesn't seem to be temporary, as management remains bullish on their near-term outlook. They expect revenues to be between $10.3 billion and $10.8 billion in the fiscal first quarter of 2027, which would mean another strong quarterly growth following a huge fiscal fourth quarter. Now that the company has signed five more New Business Model agreements, revenue growth and profitability will surely improve.\\nNVIDIA's AI Demand Drives Strong Growth and Margin Expansion\\nThe Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\\nNVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago. Strong operating performance helped NVIDIA convert robust revenue growth into solid earnings growth.\\nNVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise. Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\\nBoth Sandisk and NVIDIA have delivered exceptional earnings growth, fueled by an increase in AI and data center demand, resulting in sharp revenue gains and substantial margin expansion. Both companies also look quite confident about their near-term outlooks.\\nHowever, Sandisk is in a highly cyclical memory business. If supply catches up with demand, memory prices may come under pressure and weigh on margins. Moreover, the company's fast-paced growth has raised expectations, which has made its growth trajectory susceptible if pricing momentum weakens.\\nSince Sandisk's business is dependent on storage demand, it has created greater concentration risk. In contrast, NVIDIA is capitalizing on the broader AI ecosystem, has dominant data center exposure, and continues to deliver exceptional AI-driven growth. To top it off, NVIDIA's 63.7% net profit margin compared with Sandisk's 56.5%, indicates its greater efficiency in converting revenues into bottom-line profits.\\nTaken together, these factors make NVIDIA a better buy than Sandisk at the moment. NVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Sandisk has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": null\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"title\": \"Dear Nvidia Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for September 24\",\n   \"publisher\": \"barchart.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor technology.\\nHuang's presence is particularly notable for Nvidia, given the company's position at the center of the global AI chip market and the ongoing U.S.-China technology relationship. Investors will be watching whether discussions around AI and semiconductor trade create any signals relevant to Nvidia's access to the Chinese market, export restrictions, or the broader outlook for AI infrastructure demand.\\nIntel vs. TSMC: One Chip Stock Controls 72.5% of the Foundry Market. The Other Is Mounting a Comeback.\\nWhile the dinner itself does not guarantee any policy changes, Sept. 24 could provide an important backdrop for Nvidia stock as the market weighs the potential implications of renewed U.S.-China engagement.\\nNvidia is a global leader in accelerated computing and AI, renowned for pioneering the GPU that revolutionized gaming, data centers, and AI-driven computing. Headquartered in Santa Clara, California, Nvidia's technology now powers everything from high-performance gaming and cloud computing to autonomous vehicles and generative AI applications. With a market cap of $5.5 trillion, Nvidia stands among the world's most valuable companies, driven by its dominance in AI infrastructure and continued innovation in next-generation chip design.\\nNvidia stock has gained steady momentum in recent sessions, with the shares now sitting just 4% below their 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. The stock is up 22% year-to-date (YTD) and 28% over the past 52 weeks, while its five-day gain stands at 7%, reflecting an acceleration in recent trading. Although in the red this morning, Nvidia has otherwise advanced in each of the past five sessions through Sept. 22, including gains of 2.5% on Sept. 17, 1.3% on Sept. 18, 2.3% on Sept. 21, and 0.66% yesterday, Sept. 22. It surged marginally in the last session, closing at $228.87.\\nNvidia's recent stock advance has been supported by several developments reinforcing the strength of the AI infrastructure cycle. The company's latest quarterly results showed revenue growth of 106%, while management has continued to signal strong demand for its next-generation AI systems.\\nMeanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong. The stock is also benefiting from renewed optimism around the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin platforms as customers continue expanding AI infrastructure.\\nIn addition, expectations surrounding the upcoming Trump-Xi engagement have added another potential catalyst, with investors watching for any developments that could improve Nvidia's ability to sell AI chips in China.\\nNVDA trades at 24.04 times forward earnings and 24.81 times sales, which is currently a premium compared to industry peers.\\nNvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\nThe company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\\nNvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\\nFurthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\\nIt's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\\nBernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon reiterated a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on NVDA stock this week while maintaining a $400 price target. The target was unchanged from Rasgon's earlier revision, when Bernstein raised it from $315 to $400 on Aug. 27, underscoring the analyst's continued confidence in Nvidia's earnings and AI-driven growth outlook.\\nFurthermore, Rothschild & Co Redburn analyst Timm Schulze-Melander maintained a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on Nvidia with a $325 price target.\\nOverall, NVDA stock has a consensus \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating. Of the 50 analysts covering it, 46 advise a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" three suggest a \\\"Moderate Buy,\\\" and one offers a \\\"Strong Sell\\\" rating.\\nThe average analyst price target for NVDA is $325.88, indicating a potential upside of 45%. The Street-high target price of $515 suggests that the stock could rally as much as 129%.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"title\": \"I've Held Nvidia for Nearly 3 Years. Here's Whether I'd Still Buy Today.\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has been one of the best stocks to own in the artificial intelligence (AI) arms race. It is up nearly 1,500% since 2023 began, providing investors with incredible returns. Unfortunately, I do not have those returns in my portfolio.\\nI bought the stock at the beginning of 2023 and watched it double. Then I sold the stock because I was worried that AI computing demand wouldn't last. I was very wrong. I had to disregard all of the prior gains and purchase shares in 2024 after it had run up some more, which has still been a great investment for me.\\nBut would I buy the stock again today? You bet I would.\\nNvidia is still a dominant force in the industry\\nNvidia is turning the investment world on its head. Normally, the law of large numbers dictates that the larger a company is, the harder it is to grow. Nvidia is the world's largest company and has reached heights that no other company has ever achieved. However, it's growing faster than any other stock among the world's top 10 companies. That's hard to believe, but it showcases just how massive the demand is for AI computing power.\\nNvidia makes GPUs , which are the go-to computing unit for all sorts of AI operations, be it training or inference. While there are alternatives, the Nvidia ecosystem cannot be beaten. Additionally, custom AI chips are starting to become more popular among many AI hyperscalers. Still, there will always be a place for computing units like Nvidia's GPUs that are flexible and can handle a wide variety of workloads.\\nDue to Nvidia's market position, it has advanced knowledge of how much computing power the AI hyperscalers are planning. The AI companies are making multiyear plans to ensure they have mapped out when they will need computing units, because data centers don't go up overnight. This gives Nvidia the ability to issue long-term guidance, and the guidance regarding the AI build-out is simply incredible.\\nNvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030. Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\\nSo, if you think you've missed the boat on Nvidia, think again. There is still plenty of upside to come, and Nvidia will be one of the biggest beneficiaries of the AI build-out. However, none of it is priced into Nvidia's stock.\\nThat's pretty much in line with where other big tech stocks trade when one-time effects don't skew their valuations, but there's one problem: Nvidia is growing far faster than any of its big tech peers. The trailing price-to-earnings ratio doesn't account for Nvidia's massive growth ahead, and that's where a forward-looking metric helps out.\\nAt less than 15 times next year's earnings, Nvidia's stock has almost no growth priced into it today. That means the stock has major upside over the next year. Furthermore, with the AI build-out expected to intensify through 2030, that leaves plenty more room for Nvidia to run.\\nEven if you've missed out on Nvidia so far, now is the perfect time to buy more. Very little growth has been priced in, and Nvidia's stock looks well positioned to be one of the best investments over the next few years.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": null\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Set For 40% Surge: 3 Catalysts That Could Drive the Next Big Move\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"NVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"fiscal 2028 growth of approximately 70%\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\\n\\\"Now, compute is revenue,\\\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\\nWhen might it happen?\\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the fiscal second quarter's 75% gross margin\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"gross margin of 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n     \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ],\n \"answers\": [\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B51\",\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported in its Aug. 26, 2026 press release for the fiscal second quarter of 2027 Data Center segment revenues of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, and consolidated revenues of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, as summarized by Zacks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release.\",\n      \"Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenues\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; per the Aug. 26 press release\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue growth, year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"117%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"consolidated revenues\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"consolidated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"consolidated revenue growth, year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"consolidated; year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% in the fiscal second quarter of 2027 from 72.5% a year earlier, as reported in the company's Aug. 26, 2026 release and summarized by Zacks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"72.5%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; year-ago quarter\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided revenues for the fiscal third quarter of 2027 to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and Zacks calculated that the midpoint implies a 12% sequential rise; the guidance was reported with the company's Aug. 26, 2026 results.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027.\",\n      \"At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenues (guidance)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"sequential revenue growth at guidance midpoint\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"12%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"calculated at the guidance midpoint\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B52\",\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B53\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia told shareholders in May that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase the Morningstar author calculates at 2,400%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": \"quarterly\",\n       \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"prior quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": \"quarterly\",\n       \"value\": \"$0.01 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"increase in quarterly dividend\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"2,400%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"author's calculation\",\n       \"party\": \"Morningstar\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration, according to the Morningstar article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Indeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share buyback program\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$80 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Morningstar article refers to Nvidia's own forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth, a rate the author says is inconsistent with the idea that the company is entering a slow-growth stage; the item does not state which period the 70% growth forecast covers.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\",\n      \"That doesn’t fit with the 70% revenue growth forecast.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"year-on-year revenue growth forecast\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Morningstar article describes Nvidia's role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments as one of the major Nvidia news items of the year; it does not break out how much of that is guarantees, credit support or direct equity investment.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"artificial intelligence investments Nvidia has a role in financing\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$500 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"as described by the article; no split between guarantees, credit support and equity investment\",\n       \"party\": null\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Indicates the scale of Nvidia's financial involvement in AI investments ($500 billion) that the annual-report disclosure of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops would quantify; the article does not separate guarantees or credit support from equity investment, so it speaks to magnitude only.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B54\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (quarter ended July 26, 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% growth reported in fiscal Q1 — with the data center business contributing $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year. The figures were reported in NVIDIA's second-quarter earnings release summarized by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total, as reported in the second-quarter earnings release\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total, versus a year earlier\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 first quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total, versus a year earlier\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data center segment, share of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total, as reported; up 126% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, about 12% above fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, according to the company's quarterly outlook cited in the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company outlook, described as about 12% above fiscal Q2 revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), describing it as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\" — with manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand the limiting factor, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth expectation\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company outlook given by CFO on the earnings call; described as supply-constrained\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's statement that fiscal 2028 growth is limited by manufacturing capacity, not demand, bears on whether NVIDIA is taking on larger purchase and supply commitments; the statement itself discloses no commitment amount.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in fiscal Q4 2027 and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect — a decline from the 75% gross margin reported in fiscal Q2 2027.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company outlook given by CFO; expected trough\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company outlook given by CFO; after price increases take effect\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Reported quarterly gross margin, as cited in the article\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"According to the article, analysts' consensus already puts NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analyst consensus (as cited by fool.com)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue consensus\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 (year ending January 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"around $412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Analyst consensus, as stated in the article\",\n       \"party\": \"Analyst consensus (as cited by fool.com)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B55\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (ended 26 July 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier and faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of the fiscal first quarter; the data center business accounted for $89 billion of the total and net income was $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended 26 July 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total; up 106% over a year earlier\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended 26 July 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data center business, part of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended 26 July 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company net income; up 126% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, about 12% above the fiscal second quarter.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company guidance; around 12% above fiscal Q2\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), calling it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\" in which manufacturing capacity, not customer demand, is the limit.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company expectation stated by CFO Colette Kress; described as a supply-constrained outlook\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The CFO's statement that growth is limited by manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand implies the company is securing more supply from manufacturing partners, which bears on the purchase and supply commitment amount that clean-2 tracks; the item gives no commitment figure and this does not establish any specific commitment.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On the same late-August 2026 earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect, down from 75% in the fiscal second quarter.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Expected bottom, stated by CFO Colette Kress\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Expected level as price hikes kick in, stated by CFO Colette Kress\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company gross margin for the quarter\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analysts' consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue (analysts' consensus)\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Analysts' consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"Analysts' consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B56\",\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue up 117% year over year to $89 billion, according to the article's recounting of reported results.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total, more than doubled year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data center segment, up 117% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal third-quarter revenue to approximately $108 billion, which the article says implies 89% growth.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately $108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company guidance, implying 89% growth\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% growth Wall Street had previously modeled.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company guidance from the CFO\",\n       \"party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth previously modeled by Wall Street\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Prior sell-side consensus model cited by the article\",\n       \"party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article estimates NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Share of revenue from customers receiving company-backed financing\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 25%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Article estimate; 'expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing'\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Company-backed financing funded off NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"potentially $175 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Article estimate of potential exposure\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The article's estimate that roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue comes from customers receiving NVIDIA-backed financing, with potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's balance sheet, bears directly on the scale of credit support and revenue backstops the thesis asks NVIDIA to disclose at its next annual report. It is an article estimate, not a company disclosure.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-6\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article says NVIDIA carries off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion, tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduce credit risk not captured by traditional valuation metrics.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Off-balance-sheet guarantees\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"exceeding $100 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Article estimate; tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"A claimed >$100 billion of off-balance-sheet guarantees tied to OpenAI-related projects is evidence on the magnitude of credit support the thesis tracks, though it is the article's assertion rather than an NVIDIA disclosure. It does not establish the exact amount NVIDIA will report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-8\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article reports that consensus earnings estimates for NVIDIA have been revised sharply higher over the past nine months, from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"and earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Sell-side analysts (consensus)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Consensus EPS estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2029\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.68 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Consensus estimate after revisions over the past nine months\",\n       \"party\": \"Sell-side analysts (consensus)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B57\",\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results (quarter ended July 26, 2026, announced Aug. 26) with record revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year from $46.7 billion and up 18% sequentially, comfortably exceeding the company's prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26.\",\n      \"Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially.\",\n      \"The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total record quarterly revenue; up 106% YoY from $46.7 billion and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance (prior guidance for the quarter)\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$91 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"prior company guidance, exceeded by actual revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported non-GAAP profitability for fiscal second-quarter 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026): net income rose 118% to $54 billion from $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, adjusted EPS rose 120% to $2.22 (above expectations), non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%, and operating income rose 124% year over year to $63.7 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations.\",\n      \"Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$54 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; up 118% from $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"adjusted (non-GAAP); up 120%, exceeding expectations\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; up from 72.5%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Operating income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$63.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up 124% YoY; GAAP/non-GAAP basis not stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's Data Center segment generated $89 billion of revenue in fiscal second-quarter 2027, up 117% year over year and representing the majority of total company revenue, reflecting demand from hyperscalers and AI companies expanding infrastructure.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; up 117% YoY, majority of total company revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, with the forecast explicitly assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China, and guided GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n      \"Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company outlook; assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"GAAP and non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The guidance's stated assumption of no Data Center compute revenue from China describes the current state of NVIDIA's China business and informs the question of whether China data-center revenue can resume under export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e8\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Street expects NVIDIA's EPS growth to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% year over year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by a further 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Street expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Street analysts (consensus)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$9.25\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst consensus; implies 102.4% YoY growth\",\n       \"party\": \"Street analysts (consensus)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.33\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst consensus; implies 65.7% YoY growth\",\n       \"party\": \"Street analysts (consensus)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B58\",\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter revenue of $96.2 billion, more than double the year-ago quarter, with data center revenue up 117% year over year to $89 billion, as reported by barchart.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion, and management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total; more than doubled year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment; up 117% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided for approximately $108 billion of third-quarter revenue, which the article says implies 89% growth.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"approximately; implies 89% growth\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, a figure far above the 45% growth Wall Street had previously modeled.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company guidance\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth previously modeled by Wall Street\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"prior consensus model, as stated by the article\",\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"According to barchart.com, NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Share of revenue from customers receiving company-backed financing\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 25%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected; customers receiving some form of NVIDIA-backed financing\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$175 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"potentially; tied to the share of fiscal 2028 revenue from financed customers\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The article's estimate that about a quarter of fiscal 2028 revenue, potentially $175 billion, comes from customers receiving NVIDIA-backed financing bears directly on the thesis's question of how much credit support and revenue backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"barchart.com reports that NVIDIA carries off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, creating credit risk.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Off-balance-sheet guarantees\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$100 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"exceeding $100 billion; tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"A reported figure of more than $100 billion in off-balance-sheet guarantees tied to named AI projects is direct evidence on the amount of credit support and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended, the quantity the thesis tracks.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts' earnings per share estimates for NVIDIA have been revised sharply higher over the past nine months, from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029, according to barchart.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts remain overwhelmingly bullish, with 45 of 50 firms rating the stock a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" a mean price target near $326 implying 42% upside from current levels, and earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Earnings per share estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2029\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.68 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"current analyst estimate, revised up over the past nine months\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B59\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (period ended July 26, 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of its fiscal first quarter — with the data center business accounting for $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, per the company's second-quarter earnings release as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter (period ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center business, of company total\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, around 12% above its fiscal second-quarter revenue, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"around 12% above fiscal Q2\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On Nvidia's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), describing it as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\" in which the limit is manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"\\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\"\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The CFO's statement that the fiscal 2028 outlook is constrained by manufacturing capacity rather than demand bears on whether Nvidia must lock in large purchase and supply commitments; the item gives no amount of such commitments, so it does not establish their size.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On Nvidia's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes take effect, down from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"bottom level\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"steady level as price hikes kick in\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"According to analyst consensus cited by fool.com, Nvidia's fiscal 2027 revenue is expected to come in around $412 billion, implying roughly 90% revenue growth for the fiscal year; the article uses those consensus figures as the base for its valuation arithmetic.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\",\n      \"Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analyst consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"around $412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"this fiscal year (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"90%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected growth\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B60\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, and non-GAAP EPS of $2.22, beating consensus by 6.29%; Data Center revenue was $89.023 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"105.85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided Q3 fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion; the article states the Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue, citing geopolitical uncertainty.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\",\n      \"Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"excludes China Data Center compute revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The exclusion of China Data Center compute revenue from Q3 guidance indicates China data-center revenue had not resumed and bears on whether U.S. and Chinese restrictions allow sales by the end of fiscal 2027.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article states NVIDIA's gross margins are expected to bottom at 71% to 72% in Q4 fiscal 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St. analyst\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St. analyst\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St. analyst\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists NVIDIA's real risks as supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Guarantee obligations\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"capped at\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States NVIDIA's supply commitments at $279 billion, directly bearing on the thesis's tracked purchase and supply commitment amount.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States NVIDIA's guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, directly bearing on the thesis's tracked guarantee and credit-support amount.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e8\",\n     \"summary\": \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA / Jensen Huang\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Fiscal 2028 growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA / Jensen Huang\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's statement that NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand and its fiscal 2028 growth outlook indicate a supply-constrained demand backdrop for the Vera Rubin ramp, though it does not establish shipment timing.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part6.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"title\\\"],a[\\\"publication_date\\\"],\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\"),\\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\")),print(a[\\\"text\\\"])) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"]>=4]' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part6.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"title\\\"],a[\\\"publication_date\\\"],\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\"),\\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\")),print(a[\\\"text\\\"])) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"]>=4]' \"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 4 Dear Nvidia Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for September 24 2026-09-23 NAMED [] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}]\nNvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor technology.\nHuang's presence is particularly notable for Nvidia, given the company's position at the center of the global AI chip market and the ongoing U.S.-China technology relationship. Investors will be watching whether discussions around AI and semiconductor trade create any signals relevant to Nvidia's access to the Chinese market, export restrictions, or the broader outlook for AI infrastructure demand.\nIntel vs. TSMC: One Chip Stock Controls 72.5% of the Foundry Market. The Other Is Mounting a Comeback.\nWhile the dinner itself does not guarantee any policy changes, Sept. 24 could provide an important backdrop for Nvidia stock as the market weighs the potential implications of renewed U.S.-China engagement.\nNvidia is a global leader in accelerated computing and AI, renowned for pioneering the GPU that revolutionized gaming, data centers, and AI-driven computing. Headquartered in Santa Clara, California, Nvidia's technology now powers everything from high-performance gaming and cloud computing to autonomous vehicles and generative AI applications. With a market cap of $5.5 trillion, Nvidia stands among the world's most valuable companies, driven by its dominance in AI infrastructure and continued innovation in next-generation chip design.\nNvidia stock has gained steady momentum in recent sessions, with the shares now sitting just 4% below their 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. The stock is up 22% year-to-date (YTD) and 28% over the past 52 weeks, while its five-day gain stands at 7%, reflecting an acceleration in recent trading. Although in the red this morning, Nvidia has otherwise advanced in each of the past five sessions through Sept. 22, including gains of 2.5% on Sept. 17, 1.3% on Sept. 18, 2.3% on Sept. 21, and 0.66% yesterday, Sept. 22. It surged marginally in the last session, closing at $228.87.\nNvidia's recent stock advance has been supported by several developments reinforcing the strength of the AI infrastructure cycle. The company's latest quarterly results showed revenue growth of 106%, while management has continued to signal strong demand for its next-generation AI systems.\nMeanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong. The stock is also benefiting from renewed optimism around the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin platforms as customers continue expanding AI infrastructure.\nIn addition, expectations surrounding the upcoming Trump-Xi engagement have added another potential catalyst, with investors watching for any developments that could improve Nvidia's ability to sell AI chips in China.\nNVDA trades at 24.04 times forward earnings and 24.81 times sales, which is currently a premium compared to industry peers.\nNvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\nThe company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\nNvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\nFurthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\nIt's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\nBernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon reiterated a \"Buy\" rating on NVDA stock this week while maintaining a $400 price target. The target was unchanged from Rasgon's earlier revision, when Bernstein raised it from $315 to $400 on Aug. 27, underscoring the analyst's continued confidence in Nvidia's earnings and AI-driven growth outlook.\nFurthermore, Rothschild & Co Redburn analyst Timm Schulze-Melander maintained a \"Buy\" rating on Nvidia with a $325 price target.\nOverall, NVDA stock has a consensus \"Strong Buy\" rating. Of the 50 analysts covering it, 46 advise a \"Strong Buy,\" three suggest a \"Moderate Buy,\" and one offers a \"Strong Sell\" rating.\nThe average analyst price target for NVDA is $325.88, indicating a potential upside of 45%. The Street-high target price of $515 suggests that the stock could rally as much as 129%.\nARTICLE 5 I've Held Nvidia for Nearly 3 Years. Here's Whether I'd Still Buy Today. 2026-09-23 NAMED [] QUESTIONS None\nNvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has been one of the best stocks to own in the artificial intelligence (AI) arms race. It is up nearly 1,500% since 2023 began, providing investors with incredible returns. Unfortunately, I do not have those returns in my portfolio.\nI bought the stock at the beginning of 2023 and watched it double. Then I sold the stock because I was worried that AI computing demand wouldn't last. I was very wrong. I had to disregard all of the prior gains and purchase shares in 2024 after it had run up some more, which has still been a great investment for me.\nBut would I buy the stock again today? You bet I would.\nNvidia is still a dominant force in the industry\nNvidia is turning the investment world on its head. Normally, the law of large numbers dictates that the larger a company is, the harder it is to grow. Nvidia is the world's largest company and has reached heights that no other company has ever achieved. However, it's growing faster than any other stock among the world's top 10 companies. That's hard to believe, but it showcases just how massive the demand is for AI computing power.\nNvidia makes GPUs , which are the go-to computing unit for all sorts of AI operations, be it training or inference. While there are alternatives, the Nvidia ecosystem cannot be beaten. Additionally, custom AI chips are starting to become more popular among many AI hyperscalers. Still, there will always be a place for computing units like Nvidia's GPUs that are flexible and can handle a wide variety of workloads.\nDue to Nvidia's market position, it has advanced knowledge of how much computing power the AI hyperscalers are planning. The AI companies are making multiyear plans to ensure they have mapped out when they will need computing units, because data centers don't go up overnight. This gives Nvidia the ability to issue long-term guidance, and the guidance regarding the AI build-out is simply incredible.\nNvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030. Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\nSo, if you think you've missed the boat on Nvidia, think again. There is still plenty of upside to come, and Nvidia will be one of the biggest beneficiaries of the AI build-out. However, none of it is priced into Nvidia's stock.\nThat's pretty much in line with where other big tech stocks trade when one-time effects don't skew their valuations, but there's one problem: Nvidia is growing far faster than any of its big tech peers. The trailing price-to-earnings ratio doesn't account for Nvidia's massive growth ahead, and that's where a forward-looking metric helps out.\nAt less than 15 times next year's earnings, Nvidia's stock has almost no growth priced into it today. That means the stock has major upside over the next year. Furthermore, with the AI build-out expected to intensify through 2030, that leaves plenty more room for Nvidia to run.\nEven if you've missed out on Nvidia so far, now is the perfect time to buy more. Very little growth has been priced in, and Nvidia's stock looks well positioned to be one of the best investments over the next few years.\nARTICLE 6 Nvidia Stock Set For 40% Surge: 3 Catalysts That Could Drive the Next Big Move 2026-09-23 NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'fiscal 2028 growth of approximately 70%'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand'}] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}]\nNVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms.\nARTICLE 8 Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion 2026-09-25 NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': \"the fiscal second quarter's 75% gross margin\"}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'gross margin of 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028'}] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {'id': 'clean-6', 'question': 'Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?', 'period': \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}]\nNvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\n\"Now, compute is revenue,\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\nWhen might it happen?\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part6.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"\\\\nANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"])\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  print(\\\"ST\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",s[\\\"statement_party\\\"],\\\"DATE\\\",s[\\\"statement_date\\\"],\\\"SUMMARY\\\",s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"LINKS\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\"))\n  for f in s[\\\"figures\\\"]: print(\\\"FIG\\\",f[\\\"n\\\"],f[\\\"measure\\\"],f[\\\"value\\\"],f[\\\"kind\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",f[\\\"party\\\"])\n' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part6.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"\\\\nANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"])\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  print(\\\"ST\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",s[\\\"statement_party\\\"],\\\"DATE\\\",s[\\\"statement_date\\\"],\\\"SUMMARY\\\",s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"LINKS\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\"))\n  for f in s[\\\"figures\\\"]: print(\\\"FIG\\\",f[\\\"n\\\"],f[\\\"measure\\\"],f[\\\"value\\\"],f[\\\"kind\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",f[\\\"party\\\"])\n' \"","aggregated_output":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 5, in <module>\nTypeError: string indices must be integers\n\nANSWER B51 ARTICLE 3\nST e1 PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA reported in its Aug. 26, 2026 press release for the fiscal second quarter of 2027 Data Center segment revenues of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, and consolidated revenues of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, as summarized by Zacks. LINKS ABSENT\nFIG 1 Data Center segment revenues $89 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 Data Center segment revenue growth, year over year 117% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 3 consolidated revenues $96.2 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 4 consolidated revenue growth, year over year 106% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST e2 PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% in the fiscal second quarter of 2027 from 72.5% a year earlier, as reported in the company's Aug. 26, 2026 release and summarized by Zacks. LINKS ABSENT\nFIG 1 non-GAAP gross margin 75% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 non-GAAP gross margin 72.5% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST e3 PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA guided revenues for the fiscal third quarter of 2027 to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and Zacks calculated that the midpoint implies a 12% sequential rise; the guidance was reported with the company's Aug. 26, 2026 results. LINKS ABSENT\nFIG 1 revenues (guidance) $108 billion guidance PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 sequential revenue growth at guidance midpoint 12% estimate PARTY Zacks\n\nANSWER B52 ARTICLE 5\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part6.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"\\\\nANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"])\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  if not isinstance(s,dict): print(\\\"UNUSUAL\\\",repr(s)); continue\n  print(\\\"ST\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",s[\\\"statement_party\\\"],\\\"DATE\\\",s[\\\"statement_date\\\"],\\\"SUMMARY\\\",s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"LINKS\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\"))\n  for f in s[\\\"figures\\\"]: print(\\\"FIG\\\",f[\\\"n\\\"],f[\\\"measure\\\"],f[\\\"value\\\"],f[\\\"kind\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",f[\\\"party\\\"])\n' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part6.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"\\\\nANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"])\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  if not isinstance(s,dict): print(\\\"UNUSUAL\\\",repr(s)); continue\n  print(\\\"ST\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",s[\\\"statement_party\\\"],\\\"DATE\\\",s[\\\"statement_date\\\"],\\\"SUMMARY\\\",s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"LINKS\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\"))\n  for f in s[\\\"figures\\\"]: print(\\\"FIG\\\",f[\\\"n\\\"],f[\\\"measure\\\"],f[\\\"value\\\"],f[\\\"kind\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",f[\\\"party\\\"])\n' \"","aggregated_output":"\nANSWER B51 ARTICLE 3\nST e1 PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA reported in its Aug. 26, 2026 press release for the fiscal second quarter of 2027 Data Center segment revenues of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, and consolidated revenues of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, as summarized by Zacks. LINKS ABSENT\nFIG 1 Data Center segment revenues $89 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 Data Center segment revenue growth, year over year 117% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 3 consolidated revenues $96.2 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 4 consolidated revenue growth, year over year 106% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST e2 PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% in the fiscal second quarter of 2027 from 72.5% a year earlier, as reported in the company's Aug. 26, 2026 release and summarized by Zacks. LINKS ABSENT\nFIG 1 non-GAAP gross margin 75% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 non-GAAP gross margin 72.5% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST e3 PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA guided revenues for the fiscal third quarter of 2027 to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and Zacks calculated that the midpoint implies a 12% sequential rise; the guidance was reported with the company's Aug. 26, 2026 results. LINKS ABSENT\nFIG 1 revenues (guidance) $108 billion guidance PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 sequential revenue growth at guidance midpoint 12% estimate PARTY Zacks\n\nANSWER B52 ARTICLE 5\nUNUSUAL 'n'\nUNUSUAL 'o'\nUNUSUAL 'n'\nUNUSUAL 'e'\nUNUSUAL ' '\nUNUSUAL 'k'\nUNUSUAL 'e'\nUNUSUAL 'p'\nUNUSUAL 't'\n\nANSWER B53 ARTICLE 2\nST ev-1 PARTY Nvidia DATE May SUMMARY Nvidia told shareholders in May that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase the Morningstar author calculates at 2,400%. LINKS none\nFIG 1 quarterly dividend per share $0.25 per share actual PARTY Nvidia\nFIG 2 prior quarterly dividend per share $0.01 per share actual PARTY Nvidia\nFIG 3 increase in quarterly dividend 2,400% actual PARTY Morningstar\nST ev-2 PARTY Nvidia DATE None SUMMARY Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration, according to the Morningstar article. LINKS none\nFIG 1 share buyback program $80 billion actual PARTY Nvidia\nST ev-3 PARTY Nvidia DATE None SUMMARY The Morningstar article refers to Nvidia's own forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth, a rate the author says is inconsistent with the idea that the company is entering a slow-growth stage; the item does not state which period the 70% growth forecast covers. LINKS none\nFIG 1 year-on-year revenue growth forecast 70% guidance PARTY Nvidia\nST ev-4 PARTY Nvidia DATE None SUMMARY The Morningstar article describes Nvidia's role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments as one of the major Nvidia news items of the year; it does not break out how much of that is guarantees, credit support or direct equity investment. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Indicates the scale of Nvidia's financial involvement in AI investments ($500 billion) that the annual-report disclosure of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops would quantify; the article does not separate guarantees or credit support from equity investment, so it speaks to magnitude only.\"}]\nFIG 1 artificial intelligence investments Nvidia has a role in financing $500 billion actual PARTY None\n\nANSWER B54 ARTICLE 8\nST ev-1 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (quarter ended July 26, 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% growth reported in fiscal Q1 — with the data center business contributing $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year. The figures were reported in NVIDIA's second-quarter earnings release summarized by fool.com. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue $96.2 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 Revenue growth year over year 106% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 3 Revenue growth year over year 85% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 4 Data center revenue $89 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 5 Net income $59.7 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST ev-2 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, about 12% above fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, according to the company's quarterly outlook cited in the article. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue guidance $108 billion guidance PARTY NVIDIA\nST ev-3 PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) DATE late August 2026 SUMMARY On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), describing it as \"a supply-constrained outlook\" — with manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand the limiting factor, according to the article. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-2', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Management's statement that fiscal 2028 growth is limited by manufacturing capacity, not demand, bears on whether NVIDIA is taking on larger purchase and supply commitments; the statement itself discloses no commitment amount.\"}]\nFIG 1 Revenue growth expectation roughly 70% guidance PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\nST ev-4 PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) DATE late August 2026 SUMMARY On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in fiscal Q4 2027 and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect — a decline from the 75% gross margin reported in fiscal Q2 2027. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Gross margin guidance between 71% and 72% guidance PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\nFIG 2 Gross margin guidance 72% to 73% guidance PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\nFIG 3 Gross margin 75% actual PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\nST ev-5 PARTY Analyst consensus (as cited by fool.com) DATE None SUMMARY According to the article, analysts' consensus already puts NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue consensus around $412 billion estimate PARTY Analyst consensus (as cited by fool.com)\n\nANSWER B55 ARTICLE 8\nST ev-1 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (ended 26 July 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier and faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of the fiscal first quarter; the data center business accounted for $89 billion of the total and net income was $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue $96.2 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 Data center revenue $89 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 3 Net income $59.7 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST ev-2 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, about 12% above the fiscal second quarter. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue guidance $108 billion guidance PARTY NVIDIA\nST ev-3 PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) DATE late August 2026 SUMMARY On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), calling it \"a supply-constrained outlook\" in which manufacturing capacity, not customer demand, is the limit. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-2', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"The CFO's statement that growth is limited by manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand implies the company is securing more supply from manufacturing partners, which bears on the purchase and supply commitment amount that clean-2 tracks; the item gives no commitment figure and this does not establish any specific commitment.\"}]\nFIG 1 Revenue growth roughly 70% guidance PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\nST ev-4 PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) DATE late August 2026 SUMMARY On the same late-August 2026 earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect, down from 75% in the fiscal second quarter. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Gross margin between 71% and 72% guidance PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\nFIG 2 Gross margin 72% to 73% guidance PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\nFIG 3 Gross margin 75% actual PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\nST ev-5 PARTY Analysts' consensus DATE None SUMMARY Analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion, according to the article. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue (analysts' consensus) $412 billion estimate PARTY Analysts' consensus\n\nANSWER B56 ARTICLE 1\nST ev-1 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA's fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue up 117% year over year to $89 billion, according to the article's recounting of reported results. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue $96.2 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 Data center revenue $89 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST ev-2 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA management guided fiscal third-quarter revenue to approximately $108 billion, which the article says implies 89% growth. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue guidance approximately $108 billion guidance PARTY NVIDIA\nST ev-4 PARTY Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO) DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% growth Wall Street had previously modeled. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue growth 70% guidance PARTY Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\nFIG 2 Revenue growth previously modeled by Wall Street 45% estimate PARTY Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\nST ev-5 PARTY barchart.com DATE None SUMMARY The article estimates NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"The article's estimate that roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue comes from customers receiving NVIDIA-backed financing, with potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's balance sheet, bears directly on the scale of credit support and revenue backstops the thesis asks NVIDIA to disclose at its next annual report. It is an article estimate, not a company disclosure.\"}]\nFIG 1 Share of revenue from customers receiving company-backed financing roughly 25% estimate PARTY barchart.com\nFIG 2 Company-backed financing funded off NVIDIA's balance sheet potentially $175 billion estimate PARTY barchart.com\nST ev-6 PARTY barchart.com DATE None SUMMARY The article says NVIDIA carries off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion, tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduce credit risk not captured by traditional valuation metrics. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"A claimed >$100 billion of off-balance-sheet guarantees tied to OpenAI-related projects is evidence on the magnitude of credit support the thesis tracks, though it is the article's assertion rather than an NVIDIA disclosure. It does not establish the exact amount NVIDIA will report.\"}]\nFIG 1 Off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion estimate PARTY barchart.com\nST ev-8 PARTY Sell-side analysts (consensus) DATE None SUMMARY The article reports that consensus earnings estimates for NVIDIA have been revised sharply higher over the past nine months, from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Consensus EPS estimate $15.68 per share estimate PARTY Sell-side analysts (consensus)\n\nANSWER B57 ARTICLE 4\nST e2 PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results (quarter ended July 26, 2026, announced Aug. 26) with record revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year from $46.7 billion and up 18% sequentially, comfortably exceeding the company's prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue $96.2 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 Revenue guidance (prior guidance for the quarter) $91 billion, plus or minus 2% guidance PARTY NVIDIA\nST e3 PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA reported non-GAAP profitability for fiscal second-quarter 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026): net income rose 118% to $54 billion from $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, adjusted EPS rose 120% to $2.22 (above expectations), non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%, and operating income rose 124% year over year to $63.7 billion. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Non-GAAP net income $54 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 Adjusted EPS $2.22 actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 3 Non-GAAP gross margin 75% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 4 Operating income $63.7 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST e4 PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA's Data Center segment generated $89 billion of revenue in fiscal second-quarter 2027, up 117% year over year and representing the majority of total company revenue, reflecting demand from hyperscalers and AI companies expanding infrastructure. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Data Center segment revenue $89 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST e5 PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, with the forecast explicitly assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China, and guided GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-3', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"The guidance's stated assumption of no Data Center compute revenue from China describes the current state of NVIDIA's China business and informs the question of whether China data-center revenue can resume under export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments.\"}]\nFIG 1 Revenue guidance $108 billion, plus or minus 2% guidance PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 Gross margin guidance approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points guidance PARTY NVIDIA\nST e8 PARTY Street analysts (consensus) DATE None SUMMARY The Street expects NVIDIA's EPS growth to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% year over year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by a further 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028. LINKS none\nFIG 1 EPS $9.25 estimate PARTY Street analysts (consensus)\nFIG 2 EPS $15.33 estimate PARTY Street analysts (consensus)\n\nANSWER B58 ARTICLE 1\nST e1 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter revenue of $96.2 billion, more than double the year-ago quarter, with data center revenue up 117% year over year to $89 billion, as reported by barchart.com. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue $96.2 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 Data center revenue $89 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST e2 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA management guided for approximately $108 billion of third-quarter revenue, which the article says implies 89% growth. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue guidance $108 billion guidance PARTY NVIDIA\nST e4 PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, a figure far above the 45% growth Wall Street had previously modeled. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue growth 70% guidance PARTY NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\nFIG 2 Revenue growth previously modeled by Wall Street 45% estimate PARTY Wall Street\nST e5 PARTY barchart.com DATE None SUMMARY According to barchart.com, NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"The article's estimate that about a quarter of fiscal 2028 revenue, potentially $175 billion, comes from customers receiving NVIDIA-backed financing bears directly on the thesis's question of how much credit support and revenue backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners.\"}]\nFIG 1 Share of revenue from customers receiving company-backed financing roughly 25% estimate PARTY barchart.com\nFIG 2 Revenue funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet $175 billion estimate PARTY barchart.com\nST e6 PARTY barchart.com DATE None SUMMARY barchart.com reports that NVIDIA carries off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, creating credit risk. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': 'A reported figure of more than $100 billion in off-balance-sheet guarantees tied to named AI projects is direct evidence on the amount of credit support and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended, the quantity the thesis tracks.'}]\nFIG 1 Off-balance-sheet guarantees $100 billion actual PARTY barchart.com\nST e7 PARTY consensus DATE None SUMMARY Analysts' earnings per share estimates for NVIDIA have been revised sharply higher over the past nine months, from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029, according to barchart.com. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Earnings per share estimate $15.68 per share estimate PARTY consensus\n\nANSWER B59 ARTICLE 8\nST ev-1 PARTY Nvidia DATE None SUMMARY Nvidia reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (period ended July 26, 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of its fiscal first quarter — with the data center business accounting for $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, per the company's second-quarter earnings release as reported by fool.com. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue $96.2 billion actual PARTY Nvidia\nFIG 2 Revenue growth year over year 106% actual PARTY Nvidia\nFIG 3 Data center revenue $89 billion actual PARTY Nvidia\nFIG 4 Net income $59.7 billion actual PARTY Nvidia\nST ev-2 PARTY Nvidia DATE None SUMMARY Nvidia guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, around 12% above its fiscal second-quarter revenue, as reported by fool.com. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue guidance $108 billion guidance PARTY Nvidia\nST ev-3 PARTY Nvidia DATE late August SUMMARY On Nvidia's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), describing it as \"a supply-constrained outlook\" in which the limit is manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand, as reported by fool.com. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-2', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"The CFO's statement that the fiscal 2028 outlook is constrained by manufacturing capacity rather than demand bears on whether Nvidia must lock in large purchase and supply commitments; the item gives no amount of such commitments, so it does not establish their size.\"}]\nFIG 1 Revenue growth roughly 70% guidance PARTY Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\nST ev-4 PARTY Nvidia DATE late August SUMMARY On Nvidia's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes take effect, down from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, as reported by fool.com. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Gross margin between 71% and 72% guidance PARTY Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\nFIG 2 Gross margin 72% to 73% guidance PARTY Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\nFIG 3 Gross margin 75% actual PARTY Nvidia\nST ev-5 PARTY Analyst consensus DATE None SUMMARY According to analyst consensus cited by fool.com, Nvidia's fiscal 2027 revenue is expected to come in around $412 billion, implying roughly 90% revenue growth for the fiscal year; the article uses those consensus figures as the base for its valuation arithmetic. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue around $412 billion estimate PARTY consensus\nFIG 2 Revenue growth 90% estimate PARTY consensus\n\nANSWER B60 ARTICLE 6\nST e1 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA reported Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, and non-GAAP EPS of $2.22, beating consensus by 6.29%; Data Center revenue was $89.023 billion. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Revenue $96.22 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 Revenue growth year over year 105.85% actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 3 Non-GAAP EPS $2.22 actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 4 Data Center revenue $89.023 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST e2 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None SUMMARY NVIDIA guided Q3 fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion; the article states the Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue, citing geopolitical uncertainty. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-3', 'new_question': False, 'reason': 'The exclusion of China Data Center compute revenue from Q3 guidance indicates China data-center revenue had not resumed and bears on whether U.S. and Chinese restrictions allow sales by the end of fiscal 2027.'}]\nFIG 1 Revenue guidance $108 billion guidance PARTY NVIDIA\nST e3 PARTY 24/7 Wall St. analyst DATE 2026-09-23 SUMMARY The article states NVIDIA's gross margins are expected to bottom at 71% to 72% in Q4 fiscal 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale. LINKS none\nFIG 1 Gross margin 71% to 72% estimate PARTY 24/7 Wall St. analyst\nFIG 2 Gross margin 72% to 73% estimate PARTY 24/7 Wall St. analyst\nST e4 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None SUMMARY 24/7 Wall St. lists NVIDIA's real risks as supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-2', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"States NVIDIA's supply commitments at $279 billion, directly bearing on the thesis's tracked purchase and supply commitment amount.\"}, {'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"States NVIDIA's guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, directly bearing on the thesis's tracked guarantee and credit-support amount.\"}]\nFIG 1 Supply commitments $279 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 2 Guarantee obligations $108.5 billion actual PARTY NVIDIA\nFIG 3 Days sales outstanding (DSO) 60 days actual PARTY NVIDIA\nST e8 PARTY NVIDIA / Jensen Huang DATE None SUMMARY Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. LINKS [{'question': 'clean-4', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Management's statement that NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand and its fiscal 2028 growth outlook indicate a supply-constrained demand backdrop for the Vera Rubin ramp, though it does not establish shipment timing.\"}]\nFIG 1 Fiscal 2028 growth approximately 70% guidance PARTY NVIDIA / Jensen Huang\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
